{"qid":"Q1049","names":["Republic of Sudan"],"title":"Republic of Sudan","endonym":{"native":"السودان","lang":"ar"},"capsule":"The Republic of Sudan achieved independence from Anglo-Egyptian condominium on 1 January 1956, becoming the first sub-Saharan and Arab League state to do so in the post-war decolonization wave. Its modern history has been dominated by recurrent civil conflict: a first civil war (1955–1972) ended with the Addis Ababa Agreement, a second (1983–2005) concluded with the Comprehensive Peace Agreement that led to South Sudan's secession on 9 July 2011, leaving Sudan without its oil-rich southern provinces. Omar al-Bashir's 30-year authoritarian rule (1989–2019), punctuated by the International Criminal Court's 2009 and 2010 indictments for genocide in Darfur, ended with a popular uprising; in April 2023 a new civil war erupted between the Sudanese Armed Forces and the Rapid Support Forces paramilitary. The dataset geometry runs from 1956 through 2024.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. The post-1956 boundaries are based on colonial administrative demarcations and are formally defined; the 2011 secession boundary with South Sudan along the Nile-watershed divide remains partly disputed (notably the Abyei region), introducing some ambiguity in southern boundary geometry.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Britannica: Sudan","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Sudan"}],"britannica":"place/Sudan","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Sudan","rulers":[{"qid":"Q319216","name":"Ibrahim Abboud","start":1958,"end":1964,"image":"Ibrahim Abboud2.jpg","imageUrl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Specia